<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        THE CONTROLS THIS WINDOW IS MADE OF, EVERYWHERE EXCEPT THE LIST - the text styles,
        the sentences the window says, the field somebody types into and the box somebody
        ticks. Not one value of its own: every number, colour and thickness below is a name
        declared in Values.xaml.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other two files.

        THIS FILE SHED THE LIST ON 2026-08-11 AND KEPT ITS NAME, because the name was always
        describing what is still here. Themes/List.xaml took the grid, the row template, the
        cell, the headings and the marks, unchanged and in the order they were in. It went
        because this file stood exactly on its 530-line ceiling and the configurable columns
        of S6d2 needed cell templates a resource dictionary could hand to a column built in
        code.

        THE media PREFIX WENT WITH THE LIST, and nothing beyond x is declared here on
        purpose. The mutation entry that proves a styles file holds no value has to inject a
        type the file can actually parse, so it uses Thickness rather than system:Double -
        which only works while neither styles file declares the system prefix.

        NEITHER THIS FILE NOR List.xaml LOADS ON ITS OWN. Both reach WPF UI's keys and both
        reach ours in Values.xaml, and StaticResource resolves them WHILE THE FILE IS BEING
        READ. So the merge order in App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then Values.xaml,
        then this, then List.xaml.
    -->
    <!--
        BasedOn by NAME on WPF UI's own styles, not on {x:Type TextBlock}.

        The type form worked while the base was the built-in Fluent theme, and would now be
        ambiguous: this dictionary is merged after theirs and is itself keyed by the same type,
        so which style it resolves to depends on lookup order rather than on intent. Their keys
        are stable, documented by their own markup - DataGrid.xaml bases four styles on exactly
        these names - and they say what is meant.

        CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW: this file no longer loads on its own. It resolves keys that only
        exist once ControlsDictionary is merged, which is why tools/wpfui-probe loads it after
        theirs. That is a real dependency and it is the shape of the decision the owner made.

        Every one of the three below keeps OUR size setter on top of their template. That is
        the density decision of 2026-08-02 in the two places it is spelled out.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A PANEL'S OWN TITLE. SemiBold since 2026-08-19, and measured rather than preferred: it set a
        size and no weight, so it arrived Normal while the sentence under it was SemiBold - the
        largest text on the panel was the THINNEST thing on it. Both panels using this key had the
        same fault, so one setter fixes both.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="HeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="FixedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SubduedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A HEADING INSIDE A PANEL, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME THING AS THE PANEL'S OWN TITLE. Added
        2026-08-19, after the owner looked at the plan panel and could not find where to start
        reading: five headings and the title were all TextSizeHeading, so nothing was the top of
        anything.

        TextSizeHeader rather than TextSizeNormal because 14 is the floor for semibold text in
        the guidance TypeScaleGuards holds, and a section heading in a wall of sentences has to
        be seen without being read.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SectionHeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHERE THE PLAN PANEL IS IN ITS SEQUENCE - nothing done, doing it, done.
        <b>NOT NoticeText, and that was the fault the owner saw.</b> This line wore the warning
        orange, which is the same colour as the line at the bottom of the window saying the
        session has no administrator rights - so the sentence telling somebody that NOTHING HAS
        HAPPENED read as something having gone wrong.

        <b>SEMIBOLD CAME OFF ON 2026-08-19, THE DAY AFTER IT WENT ON, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY.</b>
        This style and SectionHeadingText above had IDENTICAL setters - same face, same 14, same
        SemiBold, same colour - so "Nothing has been done" was rendered exactly like "In this
        order:". The panel had four headings and no body. Fixing the colour fault above created a
        weight fault here, which is the shape rule 11 of the project notes is about: a correction is
        a claim and needs checking as one.
        Size still separates it from the body at 13 and position separates it from everything -
        it is the only line between the title and the sections. Weight is what it gives up, because
        weight is now the only thing that says "this is a section label".
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanNoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE STEP OF A PLAN, AND UNTIL 2026-08-19 IT HAD NO STYLE OF ITS OWN. It was built on
        SubduedText, which is TextSizeSmall - so what will happen to the machine was rendered at 12
        under a section heading at 14 SemiBold. The label was larger and heavier than the thing it
        labelled, and the eye slid off three bold labels without stopping on any content.
        The subdued FOREGROUND is kept and the subdued SIZE is not: a step nobody asked for should be
        dimmer rather than smaller, because `C2` wants those findable at a glance and a size
        difference makes them hard to read rather than easy to skip.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanStepText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE COUNT, WHICH MOVED OUT FROM UNDER THE LIST AND STOPPED BEING SUBDUED. Complaint 8 of
        the eleven says it was the least visible thing on the screen and is the main answer to
        every keystroke - small, grey and at the bottom is three ways of saying "ignore me".

        SEMIBOLD WHEN THE LIST IS NARROWED, AND THAT IS COMPLAINT 7 RATHER THAN DECORATION. That
        one says you cannot see THAT a filter is on. The words already say it - "108 of 810"
        against "810" - but a weight change is seen without reading, which is the difference
        between a signal and a fact you have to go and check.

        TextSizeHeader rather than TextSizeHeading: 14 is the floor for semibold text in the
        guidance TypeScaleGuards holds, and this line is semibold half the time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CountLine" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />

        <!--
            Missing until 2026-08-10, and the count was black on a dark window. A regression from
            slice 5, where it "stopped being grey" - the brush was removed rather than replaced,
            so this style declared type and no colour and inherited one nobody chose. Backlog 145.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.Narrowed}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The sentence in the middle of an empty list. Larger than the rows, because it is the only
        thing on that half of the screen and somebody is looking at it wondering what happened.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="EmptyStateText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        What the result has to admit about itself, and what is wrong with the question. Two
        styles rather than one, because they answer different questions and a person glancing
        at the window has to be able to tell "your query is broken" from "your query worked and
        the answer has holes in it" without reading either sentence.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="NoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ProblemText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The one field. Its padding is named rather than left to the framework because the hint
        sitting behind it has to line up with the text a person types, and the only way to be
        sure of that is for both to come from this line.

        BasedOn is the whole reason this comment is long. Without it a default style here does
        not ADD to the base one, it REPLACES it - so the field came out white, with grey hint
        text, on a dark window. It shipped that way from S6a until 2026-08-02, through a
        screenshot review that had the field on screen.

        Found by accident, while photographing this window to compare it against a control
        library. That is the fourth time in this project that XAML has bound correctly and
        painted something other than what it promised, after the empty list from internal view
        models, the column headers outside the visual tree, and the dead DataGridRow background.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultTextBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="CheckBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE COLUMN IN THE LIST OF COLUMNS SOMEBODY CHOOSES FROM - `A8`, arriving 2026-08-11.

        A MENU RATHER THAN A PANEL OF OUR OWN, AND THAT IS THE CHEAPEST HONEST SURFACE IN THIS
        WINDOW. A floating panel needs a fill, an edge and both of their contrast ratios argued in
        ContrastGuards, for a thing that is on screen while somebody clicks four times a year. A
        menu is already themed by the control library, is already used by this window for copying a
        name, and was already looked at by a person on the release build. Nothing new is declared
        here and no colour is invented.

        BasedOn OR THE LIBRARY IS INSTALLED AND INVISIBLE FOR THIS CONTROL - `docs/10` trap 4. An
        explicit style REPLACES an implicit one rather than adding to it, so without this line the
        items would come out in the built-in Windows look inside a Fluent window.

        BasedOn ON THE TYPE RATHER THAN ON A NAME, AND THAT IS MEASURED. Every other BasedOn in
        this product names one of their keys, so this one was written DefaultMenuItemStyle by
        analogy - and there is no such key. WindowGuards went red at once with the resource name in
        the message, which is the whole reason that guard builds a window rather than trusting a
        compile. Their assembly declares DefaultMenuStyle, DefaultToolTipStyle, DefaultButtonStyle
        and eighty others, and no named style for a menu ITEM: it is registered against the type.
        So the base is asked for the same way the framework asks for it.
    -->
    <!--
        WHAT THIS COSTS AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTED: an implicit style is looked up by type at merge
        time, so this line goes quiet rather than red if the library ever stops registering one.
        A named key throws. Nothing here can be named, so the honest arrangement is to say so.

        StaysOpenOnClick, because turning on four columns is one errand rather than four. A menu
        that shuts after every tick makes a person open it four times to do one thing.

        Not one appearance value here, which is why this style is allowed to say nothing about how
        it looks: what a column is CALLED, whether it is on, and whether it may be turned off are
        all answered by the choice it is bound to.
    -->
    <!--
        A CHIP THAT IS ON HAS TO LOOK ON FROM ACROSS THE ROOM - backlog 163, and it was recorded as
        a decision rather than an oversight: on 2026-08-11 Controls.xaml stood exactly on its own
        ceiling, so a chip style would have forced a second split of the theme inside one session.
        The theme has been split since, so the reason is spent.

        WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE DEFAULT. WPF UI's ToggleButton marks "checked" with a fill a shade
        lighter than "not checked", against a window that is already dark. Whether a filter is on is
        the single thing this row exists to say, and saying it in a shade is the same failure as
        `docs/11` complaint 1 - the most important state on screen carried by the least visible
        difference.

        SurfaceSelected AND TextOnSurface RATHER THAN A NEW PAIR. Those two are the row selection
        colours - already declared, already carrying a contrast threshold `ContrastGuards` enforces,
        and already meaning "this one is picked" everywhere else in this window. A new brush would
        be a new threshold to argue for a meaning the theme already has a colour for.

        BasedOn THEIRS, WHICH IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10` OBEYED RATHER THAN AVOIDED: their style sets
        OverridesDefaultStyle and supplies a whole template, so a style of our own that did not
        inherit it would silently throw away every other state - hover, pressed, disabled, focus.
        What we override is three brushes it template-binds.

        AND IT IS CHECKED ON THE PIXEL, not on the property. The same library hardcodes a
        background inside its DataGrid cell template, where a Background setter is dead
        unconditionally - so "the setter is there" is not evidence about the screen.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="FilterChip" TargetType="ToggleButton" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultToggleButtonStyle}">
        <Style.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
            </Trigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE FACET: ITS NAME, THEN ITS CHIPS. Here rather than inside MainWindow.xaml because what a
        chip looks like is decided four lines above, and a template drawing chips is the same
        subject - the window was carrying a nested ItemsControl whose only job was to apply the
        style declared here. It also kept the window under the markup ceiling without shortening a
        single reason, which is what a seam is for.

        The hint sits on every group label rather than once beside the row, because the sentence is
        about the boundary BETWEEN groups and a person reads whichever label they arrive at. It is
        a tooltip rather than a second line: it explains a mechanism somebody discovers by clicking
        once, which is worth having and not worth a line of a window whose job is 810 rows.

        THE GROUP LABEL IS WHAT COMPLAINT 7 ASKED FOR, and it waited for this row on purpose.
        docs/11 closed that complaint in half on 2026-08-05 and said the other half - a label over
        the controls - was deliberately not built, because A5 was going to arrive and rewrite the
        row. It has, so the label arrives with the row it labels rather than being invented twice.

        THREE GROUPS SINCE 2026-08-12. Why these three and what each one may contain is decided in
        FilterChips.cs, with the measurement that settled the boundary - it is not repeated here.
        The single "Filters" label is gone: it named the row rather than any question in it.

        THE LABELS LIVE INSIDE THE CHIPS CONTAINER ON PURPOSE. tools/window-journey tells the labels
        of controls apart from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by
        asking whether the chips' ItemsControl is an ancestor. A label outside it would arrive as
        one of the window's sentences.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="FilterGroupTemplate">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="{StaticResource MarginGroup}">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label}"
                       ToolTip="{Binding Hint}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Chips}">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <WrapPanel />
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>

                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate>
                        <ToggleButton Margin="{StaticResource MarginChip}"
                                      Style="{StaticResource FilterChip}"
                                      Content="{Binding Label}"
                                      ToolTip="{Binding Member}"
                                      IsChecked="{Binding IsOn, Mode=TwoWay}" />
                    </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
        </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        The heading over one group of columns in the picker. Subdued, unticked and NOT ENABLED, so
        it reads as a divider with a name rather than as an eighteenth choice - and so the keyboard
        walks past it rather than stopping on something that does nothing.

        A MenuItem rather than a group header, and that is the repair described in
        ColumnEntryStyles: grouping a menu takes its items out of the automation tree entirely.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnHeadingItem" TargetType="MenuItem">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource MarginGroupHeading}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ColumnChoiceItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="IsCheckable" Value="True" />
        <Setter Property="IsChecked" Value="{Binding IsShown, Mode=TwoWay}" />
        <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding MayHide}" />
        <Setter Property="StaysOpenOnClick" Value="True" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        EVERY TOOLTIP IN THIS WINDOW WRAPS - backlog 167, and it is the third answer to a question
        the owner asked twice.

        WHAT WAS WRONG. CellText binds a cell's tooltip to its own text, which is how a truncated
        cell can still be read in full. Three columns carry lists - dependencies, triggers,
        required privileges - and the longest privilege list on this machine holds 28 names, about
        700 characters. A tooltip does not wrap on its own, so it arrived as ONE LINE ACROSS HALF
        THE SCREEN: the control that exists to make a long value readable was the least readable
        thing on the screen.

        BasedOn IS NOT OPTIONAL AND THAT IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10`. A style with a TargetType the
        control library also styles REPLACES theirs outright rather than adding to it - so a
        tooltip declared without this line would lose their background, border and shadow and come
        out as black text on nothing. DefaultToolTipStyle is the name they give it.

        IMPLICIT RATHER THAN KEYED, so it reaches the ones nobody would remember to opt in: the
        cells, the marks in the start column, the search box's own tip. A keyed style would have
        wrapped the cells and left the eleven others exactly as they were.

        THE TEMPLATE IS WHAT ACTUALLY WRAPS. MaxWidth alone does not - a tooltip whose content is
        a string measures that string on one line and then gets clipped, which looks like the
        width being ignored. The content has to be presented by something that knows how to break
        a line, so it is a TextBlock with TextWrapping.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="ToolTip" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultToolTipStyle}">
        <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="{StaticResource WidthToolTip}" />

        <Setter Property="ContentTemplate">
            <Setter.Value>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                </DataTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
